<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Hiraab Imamate</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1880 CE (approx.) · capital Mogadishu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Somali clan state that rose on the ruins of the Ajuran Sultanate and dominated Mogadishu and the central Somali coast for nearly two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the late 17th century the Hiraab clans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawiye">Hawiye</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abgaal">Abgaal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habar-gidir">Habar Gidir</a>, revolted against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate</a>. The Hiraab established an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> drawn from the Abgaal and military leadership from allied lineages, taking control of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-mogadishu">Mogadishu</a> and the coast north of it.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> was a clan confederation rather than a bureaucratic state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> held religious and political authority, mediated disputes under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> customary law and Islamic law, and drew revenues from the coastal trade in livestock, grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-mogadishu">Mogadishu</a> itself was split between rival quarters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shingani">Shingani</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamar-weyne">Hamar Weyne</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> weakened in the 19th century as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-the-geledi">Sultanate of the Geledi</a> rose inland and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar</a> asserted authority over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-mogadishu">Mogadishu</a> in the 1870s. The area later became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/italian-somaliland">Italian Somaliland</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hiraab-imamate.jpg" alt="Hiraab Imamate" width="320" height="176"><figcaption>Image: Replayerr · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_used_Mogadishu_according_to_a_Portuguese_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1880 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mogadishu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Somali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Somalia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Imamate of Hiraab, Hiraab</dd></dl></aside></div>
